## 7. Intrusion timestamps & footage clips

Every intrusion now records **when the object was first seen and when it left**,
plus a short video clip of the event.

### Timestamps

- The pipeline tracks `first_seen` and `last_seen` per track id.
- The **live alert** (pushed instantly over WebSocket) carries `ts` (the moment
  of crossing) and `ts_start` (when that object was first seen).
- The **completed incident** (stored when the event ends) carries `ts_start`,
  `ts_end`, and `duration` — the full "from when to when" window.

### Clips

`ClipRecorder` keeps a rolling **pre-event buffer** (default 3s) so footage
starts just *before* the crossing. On the first trigger it opens a writer and
flushes the buffer; it keeps appending frames (throttled to a target fps so the
clip plays at real-time speed) until the triggering object has been gone for
`linger_secs` (default 2s) or `max_secs` (default 20s) is reached.

- Clips are written as **H.264 .mp4 via imageio/ffmpeg** when available (plays
  in any browser); if that's unavailable it falls back to OpenCV `mp4v`. If
  neither works, the incident is still recorded with its snapshot + timestamps,
  just without a playable clip.
- Clips are downscaled to `clips.max_width` (default 640px) to bound disk/CPU.

### Where it shows up

Completed incidents appear in the **Incident History** panel with a thumbnail,
the `start → end` time, duration, and a **▶ Footage** button that opens the clip
in a modal player. History is served from `GET /api/events` (persisted to
`logs/events.jsonl`, so it survives restarts) and updated live as incidents
complete.

### Config (`clips` section)

| Key            | Default | Meaning                                   |
|----------------|---------|-------------------------------------------|
| enabled        | true    | Master switch for clip recording          |
| fps            | 15      | Clip playback fps / sampling rate         |
| prebuffer_secs | 3       | Seconds of footage kept before the event  |
| linger_secs    | 2       | Stop after the object is gone this long   |
| max_secs       | 20      | Hard cap on a single clip's length        |
| max_width      | 640     | Downscale width for recorded clips         |

### Caveats

- Clip timing is **real-time for live cameras (RTSP/webcam)**. For fast-read
  **file** sources used in the demo, footage is captured at wall-clock pace but
  the source is read faster than real-time, so file clips may look slightly
  time-compressed. Production RTSP feeds play back accurately.
- `imageio[ffmpeg]` downloads a static ffmpeg binary on first use. On an offline
  deployment, install a system ffmpeg and point `IMAGEIO_FFMPEG_EXE` at it (or
  rely on the OpenCV `mp4v` fallback).
